\contentsline {deleted}{Deleted: \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{ do not operate in a vacuum: they}}{4}{section*.39}%
\contentsline {deleted}{Deleted: \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{ The same coconut tree clip, for instance, might be posted in an explicitly political thread with a caption attacking Harris, but it might also circulate in more casual ``cringe compilation'' or fan-edit contexts where the focus is on entertainment rather than electoral choice.}}{4}{section*.40}%
\contentsline {deleted}{Deleted: \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{ A teenager who occasionally encounters a Trump ``Fake News'' meme in a non-political group chat might chuckle and scroll on, while a committed partisan in a politicized TikTok bubble may interpret the same image as a rallying symbol or an unforgivable insult. From this perspective, partisan memes might function less as tools of mass persuasion and more as mechanisms for maintaining already polarized communities.}}{4}{section*.41}%
